WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:15.336
Mothers always worry about what their children are thinking.

2
00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:20.335
Sometimes, they imagine traveling through the minds of their sleeping children.

3
00:00:20.336 --> 00:00:24.347
They like to tidy up their children's thoughts for the next morning.

4
00:00:24.348 --> 00:00:27.495
It is like straightening up a chest of drawers.

5
00:00:27.496 --> 00:00:32.218
A mother folds her child's naughtiness into a small place in the back of the drawer.

6
00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:38.584
The better thoughts are kept in the front of the drawer, ready to be brought out the following morning.

7
00:00:38.585 --> 00:00:45.780
When Mrs. Darling traveled through her children's minds, she found the words "Peter" and "Neverland."

8
00:00:45.781 --> 00:00:50.764
Though she couldn't quite place the names, they seemed strangely familiar to her.

9
00:00:50.765 --> 00:00:54.596
Mrs. Darling thought they might be from her own childhood.

10
00:00:54.597 --> 00:01:00.233
But now that she was a grown-up, it was hard to remember exactly what the words meant.

11
00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:04.894
She vaguely recalled that Peter was a little boy who lived with the fairies.

12
00:01:04.895 --> 00:01:09.808
He lived on an island called Neverland, the land of dreams.

13
00:01:09.809 --> 00:01:13.268
Every child dreams about adventures in Neverland.

14
00:01:13.269 --> 00:01:18.446
But Mrs. Darling, as an adult, knew that these adventures were never true.

15
00:01:18.447 --> 00:01:25.479
One morning, Mrs. Darling found some stray leaves on the nursery floor just inside the nursery window.

16
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.272
"How did these leaves get here?" she asked the children.

17
00:01:30.273 --> 00:01:35.358
"Some nights, Peter comes to visit," Wendy replied.

18
00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:40.751
"Peter is very naughty. He never cleans up after himself.

19
00:01:40.752 --> 00:01:46.020
He probably dropped the leaves and forgot to pick them up before he left."

20
00:01:46.021 --> 00:01:52.360
When Mrs. Darling asked Wendy to tell her more about Peter, Wendy described his visits.

21
00:01:52.361 --> 00:01:55.739
"He comes in through the window of our nursery.

22
00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.291
He sits at the foot of my bed and sometimes plays his flute."

23
00:02:01.292 --> 00:02:06.981
Unfortunately, Wendy explained, she was always asleep during Peter's visits.

24
00:02:06.982 --> 00:02:09.742
She didn't know how she knew about them.

25
00:02:09.743 --> 00:02:13.716
She just knew that Peter had surely been there.

26
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:17.393
Mrs. Darling thought Wendy was only describing her dreams.

27
00:02:17.394 --> 00:02:22.029
The nursery was on the third floor and a very long way from the ground.

28
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.143
How could anyone possibly come through the window?

29
00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:27.901
This story couldn't be real.

30
00:02:27.902 --> 00:02:31.922
But the leaves that she found on the nursery floor were certainly real.

31
00:02:31.923 --> 00:02:34.710
Mrs. Darling looked at them carefully.

32
00:02:34.711 --> 00:02:39.416
They were unusual leaves, certainly not found anywhere in England.

33
00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:43.770
The veins of the leaves formed a fine skeleton of lines.

34
00:02:43.771 --> 00:02:46.848
The following evening was Nana's night off.

35
00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:53.152
While the dog went out on the town, Mrs. Darling bathed her children and tucked them into bed.

36
00:02:53.153 --> 00:02:59.227
Then she sang to Wendy, John, and Michael, until they each fell asleep.

37
00:02:59.228 --> 00:03:03.379
Mrs. Darling sat down by the fire in the nursery and did her sewing.

38
00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.481
She loved to watch the children while they slept.

39
00:03:06.482 --> 00:03:11.707
They looked so peaceful. She, too, soon fell asleep.

40
00:03:11.708 --> 00:03:16.041
As she slept, Mrs. Darling had a dream about Neverland.

41
00:03:16.042 --> 00:03:20.433
She dreamed that a strange boy had flown far away from his home.

42
00:03:20.434 --> 00:03:23.754
What a strange dream for a grown-up to have!

43
00:03:23.755 --> 00:03:27.792
All of a sudden, the nursery window blew open.

44
00:03:27.793 --> 00:03:33.995
A boy dressed in the same skeleton leaves flew in, lightly touching down on the floor.

45
00:03:33.996 --> 00:03:38.978
Following him was a strange light no bigger than someone's fist.

46
00:03:38.979 --> 00:03:42.712
The light darted around the room as if it were alive.

47
00:03:42.713 --> 00:03:46.008
It was light as dust—fairy dust.

48
00:03:46.009 --> 00:03:50.243
Mrs. Darling recognized the boy. He was Peter Pan.

49
00:03:50.244 --> 00:03:55.313
And he was just as young as he had been when she first saw him as a little girl.

50
00:03:55.314 --> 00:04:00.627
She remembered him now. His tiny teeth sparkled like small pearls.

51
00:04:00.628 --> 00:04:04.734
At the sight of him, Mrs. Darling let out a scream.

52
00:04:04.735 --> 00:04:10.409
Just then, Nana returned from her evening out and burst into the nursery, barking.

53
00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:15.612
She growled and sprang at Peter, who leapt lightly up to the window.

54
00:04:15.613 --> 00:04:20.497
But before he could escape, Nana grabbed Peter's shadow in her mouth.

55
00:04:20.498 --> 00:04:25.516
As Peter flew away, his shadow tore off from his body.

56
00:04:25.517 --> 00:04:30.916
Concerned for Peter, Mrs. Darling ran outside and down the street to look for him.

57
00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:37.800
But all she saw was a shooting star in the sky.
